at the end of october and early november  there was a summit between mgm and  amazon executives and high up on the  agenda  the return of stargate  welcome to scitrek your sci-fi tv movie  channel  so at the end of october and early  november there was a series of meetings  between mgm executives and amazon  executives now apparently according to  my sources mgm a lot of these meetings  were about finance stuff they weren't  particularly interested the sale of mgm  to amazon has been  dragged through the courts and the  regulators and slowed down they weren't  expecting to take this long and  basically mgm is looking for direction  on what amazon expects it to do with  upcoming projects  they know that there are contracts that  need to be signed that basically amazon  are eventually going to have to pay for  so they're looking for some idea of what  amazon would like them to do they're  kind of in a bit of a limbo at the  moment of mgm because  other than projects that were already  green lit they're having to hold off on  lots of things they were planning to do  stargate included  so  there's a lot of meetings about how they  want them to do that and also a lot of  meetings about boring financial stuff  but there are also a lot of meetings  with jennifer salk from amazon and mark  burnett from mgm now these are the  really two big hitters when we're  talking about tv and movies at the two  respective companies a lot of their  meetings were about planned content  about the things that amazon intend to  green light immediately as soon as the  sale goes through  now the three main agenda points  apparently for those meetings  were the mgm back catalog  and consolidating all of that material  as much as humanly possible onto one  platform amazon prime  and getting it off other platforms  immediately now mgm don't own soul  rights for a lot of their properties a  lot of movies and tv shows are  co-produced with other companies but the  ones that they do own sole rights for  amazon fully intend consolidating all of  that onto amazon prime as quickly as  humanly possible  now the second big thing on the agenda  is james bond  there's going to be a reboot of the  james bond series there's lots of  different ways that could go and amazon  are very interested to make sure that  mgm know exactly where they wanted to go  third on the agenda is stargate and  getting that back onto our tv screens  now the main reasons why these are the  three areas that they want to discuss is  because obviously the back catalog  makes amazon a lot more legitimate now  they've been making movies and things  for a little while now they're seen as  the new boys on the block and they're  not really taken very seriously  mgm is one of the great old film studios  they're part of the great movie history  you know some of their back catalog  titles include things like ben hur and  gone with the wind and hot tub time  machine  you know some great movies  and you know they've got some of the  classics and that's the main reason why  amazon are buying them so getting their  back catalogue  consolidating to one place is high up on  their agenda  james bond financially speaking and  really critically speaking is their  biggest franchise you know  if you look at their top 100 out of the  top 20 or so  several of them are james bond movies  and i'm pretty sure that the highest  grossing movie is a james bond film live  double check but if we're talking about  high grossing movies their 10th highest  grossing film in history for mgm is the  original star game movie james bond is  the most profitable franchise they have  but second is stargate even though it's  not been on our tv screens for years now  it's still the second highest franchise  i own so amazon have come in  and basically gone what are the three  areas we can make money  the back catalog stargate james bond so  of course it makes sense that they're  going to have those conversations  already they're really coming in with a  five-year business plan but the things  that they want to prioritize are the  things that they can make the most  cha-ching on now you can look at that  two different ways really now the cynic  in me says they're just looking at the  things that are going to make them money  but really it kind of makes sense in  that they're looking at the things that  are the most valuable because they're  the things that the most popular  the reason why stargate still makes  money is because people still watch it  the reason why james bond makes money is  because of the highest grossing film of  this year already and they always make  money  people watch james bond year after year  after year  so  of course amazon are looking at their  highest most valuable franchises so how  do i know that this summit actually  happened so i was approached um a couple  of months ago now after a video i did  that that was very popular 100 000 views  on this video um i was approached by  somebody that works at mgm now he's not  high up he's not one of the big  executives or anything he's quite low  down on a pecking order um but through  that person i've been able to make a few  other contacts i've started to get an  idea of how mgm employees actually feel  about this acquisition and i can tell  you now they're not happy  the main reason why they're willing to  talk to me is because they want to have  a moan and they want to have a vent  about it and they want me to go in here  and tell you about it they want me to  tell you that they're not happy  amazon are really coming in as bully  boys apparently a lot of these meetings  hasn't been a mutual coming together of  mines of the mgm talent and the new  amazon owners it's been amazon coming in  and telling mgm executives exactly what  is going to happen  it's not been a two-way conversation  it's been bosses telling employees  a lot of mgm staff feel that amazon  aren't really that excited about the  talent that works for mgm at the moment  and their word for their jobs  they're also worried about the  intellectual properties that they own  and how amazon intends to use them one  of the things that's been raised to me  again in the last couple of days is that  brad wright is being frozen out of  stargate that amazon don't really like  the direction that brad white was  planning to take um stargate  another employee though has said that  he's actually thinks it's really good  thing that covert hit when it did in a  way because mgm were going to go ahead  full steam ahead with brad wright's  version of stargate and amazon would  probably have pulled the plug we might  have seen one or two seasons of what  brad wright had planned but then amazon  may have cut it  he also thinks that amazon to be honest  even though he's not a massive fan are  bringing a really big financial might to  the mgm studio and that he says if  nothing else they're going to get well  funded so hopefully when the stargate  franchise does re-emerge it's going to  be much better funded than it would have  been under mgm so that's a two-edged  sword he's to be fair though that person  was the only one that's actually said  anything positive about the amazon  takeover everything else everybody else  i've spoken to seem very very worried  one of the things they have talked at  this summit as well is about the time  frame for stargate's return now they  expect a movie to be on amazon prime  within 18 months of the sale being  finalized and they expect the sale to be  finalized in april or may 2022  now i think that will basically mean  that we will see a movie on amazon prime  of stargate by christmas 2023 or the  latest early spring 2024.  that will then be followed by a short  run tv series 10 or 12 episodes with one  overarching storyline  basically what we're used to seeing now  on streaming services like star trek  discovery and so on and so on 10 12  episodes one storyline  we're going to see a series every year  now even 